$83AF c ReadTrackLine ; read one line of a track
> Input: HL = the track, B = the channel (1-3), C = the attenuation, D = the transposition, E = the instrument, IXL = the note. Byte bit 0 set: a note (bits 1-7), nothing else. Bit 0 clear, bits 1-7 below $60: a note with a second byte: bit 0 the attenuation follows in bits 1-4, bit 7 an instrument byte follows, bit 6 a slide byte. $60 + k: a command: 0 a wait byte, 1 a rest to the pattern's end (wait 255), 2 the attenuation byte, 3 the slide byte, 4 both, 5 off (instrument 0), 6 a digidrum byte (then off), 7-31 a wait of k - 7 lines. Output: HL past the line, IXL = the note + the transposition, C, E, IYH = 0 a new note (the instrument restarts) or 1, IYL = the empty lines after this one, HL' = the slide; returns with the alternate set in use. 142 runs in the intro window (35 + 12 + 95 for tracks A, B, C).
$83B3 ; bit 0 set: a note alone
$83B5 ; $60 and above: a command
$83B9 ; a new note
$83BA ; + the transposition
$83BD ; the second byte
$83C8 TrackTestInstr ; bit 7: an instrument byte
$83CE TrackTestSlide ; bit 6: a slide byte
$83D2 TrackSlide ; the slide byte, sign-extended into HL'
$83DC SlideDone
$83E0 NoSlide ; HL' = 0, no wait
$83E8 TrackNoteOnly ; a one-byte note
$83F0 TrackCommand ; IYH = 1: no new note
$8402 ; 7-31: a wait of A - 7 lines
$8411 CmdWaitByte ; $C0 n: n empty lines
$841A CmdVolume ; $C4 n: the attenuation
$8424 CmdVolumeSlide ; $C8 n s: the attenuation and the slide
$8428 CmdRest ; $C2: no change to the pattern's end
$8430 CmdDrum ; $CC n: a digidrum on this channel, then off
$8439 CmdOff ; $CA: instrument 0 from this line (IYH = 0, E = 0)
$8444 c InstrumentLine ; one line of an instrument: the channel's period, volume, noise and mixer bits
> Input: HL = the line, A = the instrument's frame counter, IXL = the note, IY = the channel's period low byte in the cache (IY+$1B the high byte, IY+$36 the volume), D' = the attenuation, HL' = Retrigger, LinePitch = the pitch sum. The line's first byte E: bit 7 clear: a soft line. Bits 0-3 the volume (minus the attenuation, not below 0); volume 0 with bit 4 clear: silent (R8 0, tone and noise off), no more bytes. Bit 4: a noise byte N follows: N bit 6 a fixed period (the word after; N bits 0-4 the noise if not 0), else N bits 0-4 the noise and N bit 5 the tone too. With the tone: bit 5 an arpeggio byte (semitones added to the note), bit 6 a pitch word (added to the period); the period = PeriodTable[note + arpeggio, at most 95] + LinePitch / 2 + the pitch. Bit 7 set: a hardware line (HardLine). Output: HL past the line, IXH = the mixer bits (bit 0 tone off, bit 3 noise off). 1698 runs in the intro window (all soft lines: no hardware line played in its three patterns).
$8448 ; bit 7: the hardware envelope
$844B ; bit 4: a noise byte
$844F ; N bit 6: a fixed period
$8454 ; noise off unless N's bits 0-4
$845B ; R6
$8460 FixedVolume ; the volume - the attenuation
$8469 FixedStore
$846E ; the fixed period into the cache
$8479 NoiseLine ; noise N; bit 5 of N: the tone too
$847D ; tone off, noise on
$8483 ; tone on as well
$848E NoiseToneStore
$8495 NoiseOnly ; the noise alone
$849E NoiseOnlyStore
$84A1 SetMixer ; IXH = the mixer bits
$84A4 SilentLine ; volume 0: tone and noise off
$84AC SoftLine ; the tone, noise off
$84B9 SoftStore
$84BF ArpAndPitch ; bit 5: the arpeggio byte
$84C7 NoArpeggio
$84C8 TestPitch ; bit 6: the pitch word
$84D2 NoPitch
$84D5 AddArpeggio ; the note + the arpeggio, at most 95
$84DD PeriodOfNote ; PeriodTable + 2 x the note
$84E9 LinePitch ; LinePitch+1: the pitch sum (PlayInstruments), halved and added
$84F2 ; into the cache
$84FA HardLine ; the hardware envelope: R8 = 16, the shape, the ratio
> E (the first byte): bit 6 retrigger R13 (only at counter 0), bit 0 tone off, bit 3 a noise byte follows, bit 4 a fixed tone word follows (and with bit 5 an envelope word), else bit 1 an arpeggio byte, bit 2 a pitch word, bit 5 (without sync) an envelope word. The second byte B: bits 0-1 the shape (8, 10, 12, 14), bits 2-4 the ratio R (the envelope period = the tone period >> (7 - R), rounded; R = 3 puts the envelope at the note's own pitch), bit 7 sync (the tone period set back from the envelope period << (7 - R), + the offset byte when bit 6). Not run in c1's intro window (SK10's instrument 4 is the only hardware one: tools/sk10.py).
$84FD ; the first frame of the line: the retrigger bit
$8504 HardMixer ; D = tone off (bit 0) and noise off (bit 3)
$850A ; volume 16: the envelope
$850E ; the shape: R13 = 8 + 2 x (B & 3)
$8519 ; bit 3: the noise byte
$8526 HardSetMixer
$8529 ; B bit 7: sync; bit 6 its offset byte
$8533 SetSyncOffset
$8538 SetSyncFlag ; HardSync+1 = 1 with sync
$853B ; the ratio x 2 into HardRatio+1
$8542 ; bit 4: a fixed tone period
$8547 ; bit 1: the arpeggio
$854F HardNoArp
$8550 HardKeepFlags ; E kept in A'
$8553 ; bit 2: the pitch word
$855D HardNoPitch
$8560 HardAddArp ; the note + the arpeggio, at most 95
$8568 HardPeriod ; the period: PeriodTable[note] + LinePitch / 2 + the pitch
$857F HardSync ; HardSync+1: 1 the tone follows the envelope
$8584 ; bit 5: the envelope period given
$8589 HardTone ; the tone period into the cache
$8590 HardRatio ; HardRatio+1: the ratio x 2
$8596 ; skip 3 x ratio bytes of the shifts: >> (7 - ratio)
$859A HardShiftDown ; JR into the seven shifts
$859C ShiftsDown
$85B2 ; rounded
$85B5 SetEnvPeriod ; R11, R12
$85BD ; sync: the tone from the envelope period
$85CA ; skip 3 x ratio bytes: << (7 - ratio)
$85CE HardShiftUp ; JR into the seven shifts
$85D0 ShiftsUp
$85E6 SyncOffset ; SyncOffset+1: the offset added (B bit 6)
$85F0 HardDone
$85F2 HardFixed ; bit 4: the fixed tone word
$85FB HardEnvGiven ; the tone, and the envelope period from the next word

@module starkos-output ; the period table, sending the registers, Stop, Init
@about SendRegisters writes the AY. The CPC's STarKos player keeps a cache of the 14 register values and the last value sent of each; here each register's value is the operand of LD A,n (AYRegN+1) and the value last sent the operand of CP n (AYRegN+3), and only a changed register is written, to $FFFD (the register number) and $BFFD (the value). Each write is followed by nine NOPs and a tenth before the next: the 28-byte rows keep the layout of a longer port sequence (the CPC writes the AY through its PPI) with the Spectrum's two OUTs in place. R13, the envelope shape, is written when it changed or when Retrigger is set (the hardware line's retrigger bit, at its first frame).
@about PeriodTable is the CPC's: note 12 is 3822 (C0 at the CPC's 1 MHz AY clock) and each note a tempered semitone higher; notes 0-11 are 0 (their periods do not fit in 12 bits). The table was not converted for the ZX's 1.7734 MHz (Pentagon 1.75 MHz): docs/09-sound.md 9.6.
@about Init takes the song's header into the player's operands and runs ResetTable (zero the register cache and counters, $FF the last-sent values and instrument speeds, OR A the loop flags), so a second Init starts the song afresh. Stop silences the AY.
$860D w PeriodTable 12 ; the tone period of notes 0-95 (the CPC's table)
> Read by InstrumentLine (PeriodOfNote, HardPeriod): $860D + 2 x the note (at most 95). 3822 at note 12 is C0 at 1 MHz; on the ZX the same period is 1.7734 times higher (9.92 semitones; 9.69 on the Pentagon's 1.75 MHz).
$86CD c SendRegisters ; write the registers that changed to the AY, then R13 if changed or retriggered
> Input: A = R7 (the mixer). H = R7, C = $FD, D = $FF, E = $BF; per register in the order R0, R1, R8, R2, R3, R9, R4, R5, R10, R6, R7, R11, R12, R13: the value (AYRegN+1) compared with the last sent (AYRegN+3), written when they differ (OUT ($FFFD) the number, OUT ($BFFD) the value) and kept. R13: also when Retrigger is not 0 (an envelope restarts at each write). 567 runs (566 frames and Stop).
$86D7 AYReg0 ; AYReg0+1: R0 to send (channel A period low, IY of channel A), AYReg0+3: R0 sent
$86E0 ; the register number to $FFFD
$86E3 ; the value to $BFFD
$86F2 AYReg1 ; R1 (IY+$1B of channel A)
$870D AYReg8 ; R8, channel A's volume (IY+$36)
$8728 AYReg2 ; R2 (IY of channel B)
$8743 AYReg3 ; R3
$875E AYReg9 ; R9, channel B's volume
$8779 AYReg4 ; R4 (IY of channel C)
$8794 AYReg5 ; R5
$87AF AYReg10 ; R10, channel C's volume
$87CA AYReg6 ; R6, the noise period
$87E5 AYReg7 ; R7 from H; AYReg7+2: R7 sent
$87FF AYReg11 ; R11, the envelope period low
$881A AYReg12 ; R12
$8835 AYReg13 ; R13, the envelope shape (AYReg13+3: the shape sent)
$8839 ; changed: write it
$883C Retrigger ; Retrigger+1: not 0, write R13 even unchanged (a hardware line's bit 6)
$8841 WriteR13
$8856 c Stop ; silence the AY
> The three volumes' values 0 and their last-sent values $FF (so they are written), R7's last sent $FF, then SendRegisters with A = $3F: tone and noise off on all channels. Run once in c1, when the intro ends ($60BA).
$8872 c Init ; take a song: its header into the player, the variables reset
> Input: DE = the song (SK10 at $8990). Header byte 6 to SpecialDrum+4 (the drum channel); bytes 9-24: the heights, linker, special list and instrument table addresses and the four loop points into HeightPointer+1, LinkerPointer+1, SpecialListPointer+1, TrackAInstrTable+1, TranspLoop+1, HeightLoop+1, LinkerLoop+1, SpecialListLoop+1; byte 25 the speed into Speed+1 and SpeedCounter+1 (the first Play reads a line); the transposition stream from byte 26. LinkerEnd = the special list; SpecialMode and SpecialModeLoop from bit 0 of the special list's first and loop entries; the instrument table copied to tracks B and C; the instrument pointers, loop and end addresses of all three channels set to instrument 0's line; NewPatternFlag = SCF; then ResetTable. Run once in c1 (frame 2182 window, the intro's $6061).
$891B ; the reset table
$891E ResetLoop ; a count (0 ends), a value, then count addresses that get the value
$8925 ResetNext
$892E b ResetTable 8 ; the variables Init resets: groups of a count, a value and the count addresses that get it
> Read by ResetLoop. Three groups: 24 operands set to 0 (the register values R0-R6 and R8-R13, the transposition, height, special and special-list waits, the instrument frame counters, the tracks' attenuations), 17 set to $FF (the last-sent values of R0-R13, so that every register is written at the first frame, and the instruments' speeds), 3 set to $B7 (OR A: the instruments do not loop); a count of 0 ends it. R7's value is not in it: SendRegisters takes it from A.
$892E ; 24 addresses get 0
$8930 ResetZeroList
$8960 ResetFF ; 17 addresses get $FF
$8962 ResetFFList
$8984 ResetLoops ; 3 addresses get $B7
$8986 ResetLoopsList
$898C ResetEnd ; 0: the end of the table
$898D b Padding 3 ; three bytes no instruction reads, up to the song

